# On The Gravitational Nature Of Time
> What if time dilation, the shape of a black hole's neighborhood, and the expansion of the Universe are all the same slope?

<Image
    src="/assets/diagrams/showcase/hero_funnel.png"
    alt="A 3D funnel showing the Flamm embedding of a static gravitational slice, shaded from cyan at the throat to violet far away."
    maxHeight="large"
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="The Flamm embedding of a point mass, $y(R)=2\sqrt{r_s(R-r_s)}$. Color shows the lapse $N=1/\gamma$: cyan where clocks run slowest, violet where space is nearly flat. Every plot on this page is regenerated by `plot_generators/gen_showcase_plots.py`."
/>

<Hint label="Read the paper">
This page is the tour. The paper is the destination: **[Absolute Time in Higher-Dimensional Geometry: Classically Simultaneous Relativity via an Unwrapped Kaluza-Klein Dimension](https://zenodo.org/records/24487055)** (Andrew Mueller, Zenodo, v4, CC BY 4.0, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.24487055). If you're on a **Mac**, the [interactive companion app](/blog/by_path/lorentz_slope_visualizer) lets you move every slider yourself.
</Hint>

## The whole idea fits in one line

Special relativity tells us that moving clocks run slow by a factor of $\gamma = 1/\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}$. The paper asks a geometric question: what if that factor isn't a mysterious property of clocks, but the **slope** of an extra, ordinary, non-compact spatial coordinate $y$?

$$ {#lorentz-slope}
\gamma = \sqrt{1 + (\partial_x y)^2}, \qquad \partial_x y = \beta\gamma = \sinh\eta
$$

That is the hyperbolic identity $\cosh^2\eta = 1+\sinh^2\eta$, read as a right triangle. The base is the distance you travel, the hypotenuse is $\gamma$ times that distance, and the height is how far you are displaced along the extra dimension. In this picture time stops being a separate axis. It becomes a **ratio of distances**, and the relativity of simultaneity becomes a plain displacement along $y$.

<Image
    src="/assets/diagrams/showcase/slope_fan.png"
    alt="Left: a fan of right triangles with a common base, steeper for larger velocity. Right: gamma, beta-gamma and 1/gamma plotted against beta."
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="Faster means steeper. As $\beta\to1$ the slope diverges, and the foreshortening factor $1/\gamma=\cos\theta$ goes to zero."
/>

It's the kind of idea that sounds like numerology until you see how much follows from it. The rest of this page is a tour of what follows.

## The lighthouse and the clock-tower

The paper grew out of a thought experiment. Let observer $B$ travel between two points $p_1$ and $p_2$ at relativistic speed $v$, while observer $A$ stays at rest at $p_1$. Put a clock at $p_2$ large enough for $A$ to watch.

The textbook answer says $B$'s own clock shows $\Delta t=\gamma\, d/v$ while the clock at $p_2$ ticks along at the inertial rate. The paper takes seriously how strange that bookkeeping is: one process, one journey, two durations. The same numbers can be organized differently, with the factor $\gamma$ attached to the distance and not to the clock, and the extra coordinate $y$ is the geometry that makes that bookkeeping consistent.

<Admonition title="Where this started" foldable>
Late one night in early 2021, while making toast, I opened a book of Einstein's lectures and a sentence about the lengths of moving rods stood out. Einstein worked in an era that assumed a static Universe, and I wanted to see what changes if you revisit those assumptions with cosmic expansion in mind. I left my software job to follow that thread full time, and Fluster was built along the way to keep the work organized.
</Admonition>

## Gravity is a tilt

Here is where it gets visual. For a static mass, the paper embeds the spatial slice around it as a surface in the higher-dimensional space, the same Flamm paraboloid you see in the hero image. At every radius that surface is tilted by an angle $\theta$, and everything else is trigonometry:

$$ {#tilt}
\sin\theta = \beta = \sqrt{\frac{r_s}{R}}, \qquad \cos\theta = N = \sqrt{1-\frac{r_s}{R}}
$$

The sine of the tilt is the local "escape-velocity" flow speed. The cosine of the tilt is the **lapse**, the rate at which clocks tick. Gravitational time dilation falls out as the foreshortening of a tilted surface. Far from the mass the surface flattens and both effects vanish. At $R=r_s$ the slope goes vertical, and the clock rate goes to zero.

<Image
    src="/assets/diagrams/showcase/tilt_profile.png"
    alt="Left: the Flamm profile with tangent segments colored by tilt, steepest near the horizon. Right: beta, the lapse N, and the slope beta-gamma against radius."
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="Left: the tilt of space at each radius, from near-vertical at the horizon to nearly flat far away. Right: the three quantities the tilt controls."
/>

## One relation, three geometries

The same slope relation draws very different shapes depending on what's sourcing it. Outside a point mass it's a paraboloid. Inside a uniform-density star it closes into a spherical cap. For the whole Universe at critical density, it becomes a single curve that goes vertical at $R=c/H$, where the flow reaches the speed of light.

<Image
    src="/assets/diagrams/ai_generated/three_geometries.png"
    alt="Left: a uniform-density star, with a spherical cap inside and a paraboloid outside. Right: the whole universe at critical density."
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="A star and a Universe from one relation. The spatial slice has zero curvature outside the star and curvature $6/L^2$ inside the cap."
/>

The interior solution also reproduces a famous limit. Inside matter the lapse is pulled down faster than the slice is foreshortened, and a star cannot be compressed below $R_\ast = \tfrac98 r_s$ before the central lapse hits zero. That is the **Buchdahl bound**, and here it appears as a statement about how steep an embedding is allowed to get.

<Image
    src="/assets/diagrams/ai_generated/interior_lapse.png"
    alt="Left: lapse inside a star compared with slice foreshortening for two stellar radii. Right: central pressure and central lapse versus stellar radius, diverging at 9/8 r_s."
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="Left: inside matter, redshift outruns foreshortening. Right: at $R_\ast=\tfrac98 r_s$ ($\gamma_\ast=3$, $\theta_\ast\approx70.5^\circ$) the central pressure diverges and the central lapse reaches zero."
/>

## Falling in and flying apart are the same relation

This is the part that tends to stop people. The Hamiltonian constraint, imported from general relativity, ties the flow speed to the enclosed mass:

$$ {#flow}
\beta^2(R) = \frac{2G\,m(R)}{c^2 R}
$$

Read it with a plain mass and it describes **infall toward a body**. Read it with the mass inside a sphere of the cosmic mean density and it describes the **Hubble flow**, $\beta = HR/c$. They are the same equation. There is even a radius where the two speeds coincide, as the plot shows.

<Image
    src="/assets/diagrams/showcase/two_flows_dark.png"
    alt="Log-log plot of flow speed against distance. Infall decreases with distance, Hubble flow increases, and they cross at a particular radius."
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="Infall (pink) and Hubble flow (blue) for a galaxy of $6\times10^{10}M_\odot$. Written as a flow of the embedding, they are one relation."
/>

At the edge of the observable Universe the numbers close in a neat way: the identity $Gm_H/(c^2R_H)=\tfrac12$ follows from the definition of critical density, which the paper calls out as a Sciama-type relation.

## Does it reproduce what we already know?

This is the question that should decide whether you keep reading. The paper is explicit that, where it can be checked, the construction **imports the static vacuum equations of general relativity** and reproduces known results. It describes these as consistency checks, not new predictions, and says any difference from general relativity lives in sectors that aren't developed yet. Within that scope:

- Orbits and the effective potential, with the ISCO at $6GM/c^2$.
- The perihelion advance $\Delta\varphi = 6\pi GM/\big(a(1-e^2)c^2\big)$, which for Mercury comes to **42.98 arcseconds per century**, matching the observed value of about 43″.
- The Painlevé–Gullstrand form of the metric, whose absolute-time slices correspond to the Flamm embedding.
- Lense–Thirring (frame-dragging) precession.
- A scalar sector that reduces to Brans–Dicke theory with $\omega=0$. The paper notes this needs a specific stress along the extra coordinate to satisfy the Cassini bound, and says so plainly.

<Image
    src="/assets/diagrams/showcase/orbit_rosette.png"
    alt="A precessing orbit drawn as overlapping purple ellipses around a pink central mass, with dashed lines marking each periapsis and a green circle marking the ISCO."
    maxHeight="large"
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="A strong-field orbit ($p=24\,GM/c^2$, $e=0.5$, six revolutions) integrated numerically. The periapsis advances about 68.7° per orbit here; the leading-order $6\pi GM/pc^2$ estimate gives 45°. Mercury's real advance is about a million times smaller, roughly 0.1″ per orbit."
/>

<Card title="Where things stand" desc="Three kinds of claims, kept clearly apart" sidebar right>

**Solid, given GR's vacuum equations:** the Lorentz factor as a slope, the Flamm and Painlevé–Gullstrand geometry, gravitational time dilation as the cosine of the tilt, and the identity between local infall and Hubble flow. <br />

**Assumed, not derived:** an absolute frame identified with the cosmic flow, and a modified-inertia hypothesis (below). The author says so directly. <br />

**Fun but unproven:** numerical coincidences collected in the appendix, labeled as such.
</Card>

## The part that sticks its neck out

The geometry above is deliberately conservative. The paper's main departure from general relativity is an explicit hypothesis, called **Hypothesis M**: inertia is Machian and changes regime near an acceleration of order $cH_0$, with a characteristic scale $g_0 \approx cH_0/2\pi \approx 1.1\times10^{-10}\ \text{m/s}^2$.

It is a hypothesis, not a derivation, and it can be wrong. The paper tests it against the Milky Way's rotation curve, and the comparison is shown here with its limits visible: the curve fitted to the data prefers $g_0\approx0.25\,cH_0$, while the unfitted $cH_0/2\pi$ prediction lands in the neighborhood of the same points. A single galaxy cannot pin down the parameter, and the paper says so.

<Image
    src="/assets/diagrams/ai_generated/hypothesis_M_fit.png"
    alt="Observed acceleration versus baryonic acceleration for Milky Way rotation-curve data, with the Newtonian line, a fitted curve and an unfitted prediction."
    centerSelf
    paddingY="medium"
    caption="Observed versus baryonic acceleration for the Milky Way (Eilers et al. data, Cautun et al. baryons). Dotted: Newtonian. Red: fitted. Dashed blue: the unfitted $cH_0/2\pi$ value."
/>

